    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: Riders With A Passenger Pre-Ride Inspection</h1>
    <p>A pre-ride inspection is a quiet five minutes that decides how the rest of the day behaves, and with a passenger aboard it stops being optional courtesy and becomes the honest obligation of whoever holds the handlebars. Every fault the inspection hunts for, soft tires, weak brakes, loose controls, glazed cables, matters twice on a two-up machine, because the machine carries twice the people and the rider answers for both. The inspection is the same checklist the solo world uses, read with a passenger stakes.</p>
    <p>This page is the inspection chapter of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster for riders with a passenger. The verified fleet prices are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, general questions are on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page, and the machine-matching decision that comes before this one is the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-a-passenger-bike-choice/">bike-choice chapter</a> of the same cluster.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">Why the passenger doubles the stakes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Tires: pressure, tread and sidewalls</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Brakes: the front-rear test</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Controls, cables and the mirrors</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Lights, horn and signals</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">The condition record and the phone</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">The five-minute two-up routine</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-8">Daily habits for a multi-day rental</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why the passenger doubles the stakes</h2>
    <p>Solo, a soft rear tire or a tired brake is an inconvenience the rider notices and manages alone. Two-up, the same fault quietly removes margin that belongs to two people: braking distances grow with the added mass, low-speed balance gets less forgiving, and the passenger has no levers, no mirrors and no vote in the moment. The inspection is the rider taking responsibility for that difference before it matters, not after.</p>
    <p>The doubling is literal in the places that count. Stopping from city speed with a passenger takes more distance than the solo habit expects, so brakes that are merely adequate alone are worth questioning with two aboard. The frame carries more load, so tire pressure and sidewall condition stop being trivia. None of this asks for a workshop, only for five minutes of honest attention at the start.</p>
    <p>There is also a fairness argument that rarely gets said: the passenger hands the rider their safety without receiving controls in return. The inspection is the visible proof that the rider took that hand seriously. Pairs who inspect together, passenger watching or photographing, tend to ride calmer all day, because both people know exactly what shape the machine was in when the day started.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Tires: pressure, tread and sidewalls</h2>
    <p>Start with the rear tire, because it carries most of the passenger. Press it hard with a thumb: a properly inflated tire resists, a soft one yields, and a tire that has been sitting under load all week may look fine while riding under pressure. Ask the shop what pressure the machine runs and when it was last checked; a good counter answers in seconds, and the question itself signals that the pair reads the machine seriously.</p>
    <p>Tread next, across both tires. Run a fingertip across the center band where city riding wears the rubber first: grooves should be visible and open, not shallow, shiny or cracked. Hanoi streets stay rideable most of the year, but painted lines, metal plates and the first minutes of rain demand tread that can actually channel water, and a worn center band is the cheapest fault to spot and the most expensive to ignore.</p>
    <p>Sidewalls finish the tire story. Look for cracks, bulges, cuts and any repair patch that sits proud of the rubber, on both sides of both wheels. A bulge is a casing on its way out, and a cracked sidewall is a slow puncture waiting for a schedule to disrupt. If anything on the tire looks wrong, ask for a different machine on the spot; tire faults are the one category where negotiation makes no sense at all.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Brakes: the front-rear test</h2>
    <p>Test the front brake first, at a standstill. Pull the lever: it should firm up within the first third of travel, feel progressive rather than spongy, and return crisply when released. A lever that pumps up after two squeezes usually means air in the line, a lever that sinks toward the bar under steady pressure is worse, and both are reasons to point at the machine and ask the counter to look.</p>
    <p>The rear brake gets a walking-pace test. Sit aboard, roll forward a body-length, and press the pedal or lever firmly: the rear should bite smoothly and hold the machine from walking pace without drama, and the rider should feel the deceleration through the seat rather than hear it as a scrape or a squeal. With a passenger aboard the rear brake does proportionally more work at city speeds, so this test matters more than most riders assume.</p>
    <p>With both brakes checked, squeeze the levers and look down at the pads and discs if they are visible: pads should have visible friction material, discs should be clean and un-scored. Drum-brake machines hide this, which makes the walking-pace test the honest substitute. Any grinding sound, any pull to one side under braking, and any lever that feels wooden rather than progressive is a full stop at the counter, not a note for later.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Controls, cables and the mirrors</h2>
    <p>Work the throttle with the machine on its stand: it should open smoothly, return closed with a snap, and leave no sticky spot anywhere in the travel. Cables age fastest at the bends, so a quick look along the handlebars for frayed outer casing or cracked grommets tells the story of how the machine was treated. Two-up riding asks the rider for finer throttle control at low speed, and a sticky cable steals exactly that.</p>
    <p>The clutch matters only on the semi-automatic tier, but on a Honda Wave or Yamaha Sirius it deserves the same smoothness test: lever pull should be even, the bite point consistent, and the return clean. Lights and kill switches get a click each, and both brake levers and the pedal should activate the brake light, since a working brake light is how Hanoi traffic behind the pair learns what the pair intends to do.</p>
    <p>Mirrors are the last control and the most personal. Stand the machine level, sit aboard with the passenger aboard, and aim both mirrors for the rider eyes: lane width behind, not elbows or sky. Mirrors aimed for a previous rider are wrong by definition, and a pair that re-aims them while the passenger watches starts the day with one small shared ritual that costs nothing and pays all day.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Lights, horn and signals</h2>
    <p>Lights are the pair announcement to the traffic. Ignition on, check the headlamp on low and high beam, the brake light under both levers, and any tail lamp the machine carries. Hanoi evenings arrive quickly and riding two-up in dusk traffic with a dead tail light is a stress that five seconds at the counter prevents completely. If the machine carries turn signals, click each side and watch for both front and rear.</p>
    <p>The horn deserves an honest press rather than a polite one. City riding leans on the horn the way walking leans on eye contact: a short beep says present, and a weak horn is nearly no horn at all. If the button feels dead or intermittent, say so; it is a two-minute fix at the shop and a fifty-minute frustration on the road.</p>
    <p>Finish the electrical story by checking that the ignition and steering lock do their jobs, and that the key comes out only where the rider expects it to. Small faults in this category strand pairs rather than injure them, which is exactly why they belong in the inspection: the cost of finding them is nothing at the counter and an afternoon on a roadside otherwise.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">The condition record and the phone</h2>
    <p>Most counters record the machine condition on a slip, and the pair should read that slip with the same eyes as the bike itself. Existing scratches, dents and worn bits should be on it; if the slip is blank while the plastics carry history, ask for the history to be written down. A condition record that matches the machine protects both sides of the counter, and a record that does not match is a quiet warning about everything else.</p>
    <p>The passenger is the natural photographer here. Four sides of the machine, the odometer, the tires, and any existing damage take about ninety seconds on any phone, and they settle any condition question at return regardless of what the slip says. It protects the pair, it protects the shop, and it costs less time than the helmet strap took to explain.</p>
    <p>The phone also records the practical details that evaporate by the second day: the shop opening hours of 09:00 to 21:00 daily at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, and the machine make and color. Pairs who keep this in one phone photo album never renegotiate memory at the return counter, which is the entire point of the record.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">The five-minute two-up routine</h2>
    <p>The routine compresses everything above into an order the pair can repeat from memory. Minute one: walk around the machine, eyes on tires, sidewalls, leaks under the engine, and anything hanging loose. Minute two: sit, squeeze, click, throttle, clutch, brakes at standstill. Minute three: roll at walking pace and test the rear brake. Minute four: lights, horn, signals, mirrors with both riders aboard. Minute five: photos, slip, and the questions the walk-around raised.</p>
    <p>The order matters less than the habit. A pair that runs the same five minutes every morning stops noticing the time it takes, in the same way nobody notices buckling a helmet. What the habit buys is certainty about what changed since yesterday, which on a multi-day rental is the single most valuable thing an inspection can offer, since most faults announce themselves as slow changes rather than sudden failures.</p>
    <p>If the routine finds something the shop should see, that is the routine working, not failing. Ask, point at the fault, and let the counter either fix it on the spot or hand over a different machine; a shop that resists either answer is telling the pair something useful about the rental before the money moves. The <a href="/motorbike-guides/motorbike-maintenance-checklist-hanoi/">maintenance checklist guide</a> covers the deeper care items shops handle between rentals.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-8">Daily habits for a multi-day rental</h2>
    <p>On a rental measured in weeks, the five-minute routine shrinks to two honest minutes, but it keeps the same skeleton: tires, brakes, lights, controls. The check that grows instead of shrinking is the eye on anything that changed overnight, a new sound, a different lever feel, a softer wall on the rear tire carrying the passenger load. Change is the signal; sameness is the all-clear.</p>
    <p>Weekly and monthly renters live with the machine long enough for habits to matter twice. Chain-driven semi-automatics deserve a glance at chain condition and a listen for dry running, the scooter tier deserves its storage bay kept clear of grit, and both tiers deserve the wash they will eventually need, since a clean machine shows its faults faster than a dirty one. The daily prices of 150,000 VND for the light tier, and weekly and monthly ladders at 700,000 VND and 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND, are listed on the cost hub.</p>
    <p>The last habit is calendar-shaped: return planning. Long rentals end at the shop counter on a specific day, and pairs who check the machine the evening before the return, run the same two minutes, and photograph it in its return shape hand back a story without loose ends. The <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-luggage-pre-ride-inspection/">luggage-audience inspection chapter</a> covers the same routine from the solo-with-load angle, and the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-luggage-traffic-preparation/">traffic preparation guide</a> picks up where the inspection ends.</p>
    <h2>Frequently asked questions about pre-ride inspections with a passenger in Hanoi</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Who inspects the bike, me or the shop?</h2>
      <p>Both, and the honest answer is that the two inspections answer different questions. The shop maintains the machine between rentals, and the rider runs the five-minute walk-around to confirm it is in the shape the day needs, asking the counter about anything the routine surfaces. Doing both is what makes the inspection worth the time it takes, which is less time than reading this answer.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How do I test the brakes safely before leaving the counter?</h2>
      <p>In two stages, neither of which needs speed. At a standstill, squeeze the front lever and the rear pedal for firm progressive pressure with a clean return. Then roll at walking pace and press the rear brake to feel smooth deceleration with no grinding or pulling. If both stages feel right, the brakes are ready for the day; if either stage feels wrong, point at it before the money moves.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Should I photograph the rental bike even if the shop documented its condition?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Ninety seconds of four-side photos plus the odometer, on your own phone, settles any condition question at return regardless of what the slip says. It protects the pair, it protects the shop, and it takes less time than the helmet strap took to explain.</p>
    </article>
